  "worked_solution": "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =2\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =15\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=6\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $z$ is not changing and $z=5,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=5xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5xy) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =5x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+5y(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =5(6)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+5(2)(-3) \\\\ 15 & =30(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})-30 \\\\ 45 & =30(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=6\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{cm}.$",
